message.reasoning:
We need to refactor main method to reduce cognitive complexity. The main method has many branches. We can extract command line parsing into separate methods, use strategy pattern for options. But we must preserve behavior.

Simplify: create enum Option with handler. But easier: extract parsing into separate method parseCommandLine(args). That method will set variables and return a CommandLineResult object containing level, settingsDirectory, portFile, restore, gui, noPlugins, noStartupScripts, scriptFile, args (modified). Also handle usage, version, etc. But usage and version cause exit. We can keep them inside main.

Also the server connection block can be extracted into method tryConnectToServer(portFile, args, scriptFile, restore). That returns boolean success? It exits on success. So we can extract.

Also the initialization of settings directory and log writer can be separate.

Also the splash screen logic.

Also the server init block.

Also the getViews etc.

But main method is huge. We need to reduce complexity to <=15. We can break into multiple methods each with low complexity.

Let's design:

public static void main(String[] args) {
    checkJavaVersion();
    CommandLineOptions opts = parseCommandLine(args);
    if (opts.exit) return; // usage or version handled inside parse
    if (opts.portFile != null && new File(opts.portFile).exists()) {
        if (handleServerConnection(opts.portFile, opts.args, opts.scriptFile, opts.restore)) {
            System.exit(0);
        }
    }
    if (!new File(opts.settingsDirectory, "nosplash").exists()) {
        GUIUtilities.showSplashScreen();
    }
    initSettingsDirectory(opts.settingsDirectory);
    Log.init(true, opts.level);
    initServer(opts.portFile);
    startApplication(opts);
}

Where startApplication handles rest: initMisc, initSystemProperties, initSiteProperties, etc. But many of those are already called in main. We can keep them but maybe group.

But we need to preserve behavior exactly. So we must call all same methods in same order.

Simplify: create methods:

private static void checkJavaVersion() { ... }

private static CommandLineOptions parseCommandLine(String[] args) { ... }

private static boolean handleServerConnection(String portFile, String[] args, String scriptFile, boolean restore) { ... }

private static void initSettingsDirectory(String settingsDirectory) { ... }

private static void initServer(String portFile) { ... }

private static void startApplication(CommandLineOptions opts) { ... }

Within startApplication, we can call initMisc, initSystemProperties, initSiteProperties, etc. But we need to preserve order: after initMisc, initSystemProperties, initSiteProperties, GUIUtilities.advanceSplashProgress, BeanShell.init, initUserProperties, initPLAF, Java14 init, initActions, initDockables, GUIUtilities.advanceSplashProgress, VFSManager.init, initPlugins, load history, etc.

We can keep that sequence inside startApplication.

Also we need to handle buffer opening, view creation, etc. That is also part of main. We can create method runStartupAndOpenFiles(CommandLineOptions opts, Buffer buffer, String splitConfig, boolean gui). But maybe keep inside startApplication.

But complexity may still be high. But we can break into smaller methods.

Goal: each method <=15 complexity. The main method will call these methods, so its complexity will be low.

Let's design CommandLineOptions class:

static class CommandLineOptions {
    int level;
    String settingsDirectory;
    String portFile;
    boolean restore;
    boolean gui;
    boolean noPlugins;
    boolean noStartupScripts;
    String scriptFile;
    String[] args; // remaining args after parsing
    boolean exit; // if usage or version printed
